Fillets: where a contact of a walked blend ends on an arc and its
parameters there are not those of the arc's point, the end is not taken
at a crossing of the contact with the arc that lies farther from the
arc's point than the contact's own end does (mitcad#2300).

The case: the rim between a loft's side and a cylinder pulled from its end
face, convex in some places and concave in others, split at the points
where the faces are tangent, each piece's rounding vanishing in a point at
both ends. A convex piece rounded with 5 alone, or with the others: the
walk is right, both contacts end at the pieces' vertices on the arcs, but
the result is invalid (BRepCheck_NotClosed, BRepCheck_UnorientableShape).
The second contact's edge had both its ends on the far vertex: its
interference's parameters were 0 to 0 instead of 0 to 154.37.

At the far end, ChFi3d_Builder::CompleteData's CompParam was given the
arc's parameter of the end (408.07) and the contact's (154.37), whose
points on the face lie 1.96e-6 apart in its parameters, more than
Precision::PConfusion. So it intersected the contact's curve on the face
with the arc's. The contact runs onto the arc (the very edge the rounding
rounds) tangentially and stops a hair short of it at the vanishing end:
the only crossing found was at the contact's other end (parameter 0, arc
157.59), and, the only candidate, it was taken.

- CompParam (ChFi3d_Builder_6.cxx): a crossing whose point on the arc lies
  farther from the arc's point at the given parameter than the contact's
  point at its given parameter does is not taken; with none left the
  existing projection of the arc's point on the contact gives the end.
  Elsewhere nothing changes: the crossing such a correction looks for lies
  at the arc's point.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2301.
